          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is measured using a device called a sphygmomanometer. It records two numbers: systolic pressure, which occurs when the heart contracts, and diastolic pressure, which occurs when the heart is at rest between beats. These measurements are expressed in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg).\n\nMultiple readings are necessary because blood pressure can vary throughout the day due to factors like activity level, stress, and even time of day. For accurate diagnosis, health guidelines recommend averaging two or more readings taken on separate occasions. A single high reading is not enough for a diagnosis because it may not reflect a person's usual blood pressure pattern. This helps distinguish between true hypertension and instances of \"white-coat hypertension,\" where a person's blood pressure is elevated in a clinical setting but normal outside of it (source: \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\" and \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
